To create a debouncing function in JavaScript, you can use the following approach. Debouncing means limiting the number of times a function can be invoked within a certain time period, and only the last invocation will be executed after the specified delay. Here's a simple implementation using JavaScript closures:

```javascript
function debounce(func, delay) {
  let debounceTimer;

  return function () {
    const context = this;
    const argumentsArray = arguments;

    // Clear any existing timer before setting a new one
    clearTimeout(debounceTimer);

    // Set a new timer to execute the original function after the specified delay
    debounceTimer = setTimeout(function () {
      func.apply(context, argumentsArray);
    }, delay);
  };
}
```

You can use this debouncing function to limit the execution of another function. For example:

```javascript
const debouncedFunction = debounce(function () {
  console.log('Debounced function executed!');
}, 1000); // This function will be debounced for 1000ms

// Calling the debounced function multiple times within 1000ms will only execute once after the delay
